{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}MRC Hinterlands — Rules{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
<h1 class="mb-4">MRC Hinterlands League — Rules Summary</h1>
<p class="text-muted mb-5">This is a summary of the core rules from the MRC Hinterlands League rulebook. Full rules are a supplement to <em>Hot Spots: Hinterlands</em>. Only pages 1–8 are essential for league play; the rest are additional options.</p>

<div class="row g-4">

    {# Building Your Force #}
    <div class="col-12">
        <div class="card">
            <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white"><h5 class="mb-0">Building Your Force</h5></div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <ul class="mb-0">
                    <li>Select a faction from the Master Unit List (<a href="https://www.masterunitlist.info" target="_blank">masterunitlist.info</a>) and choose starting units from that faction's MUL.</li>
                    <li>Start with <strong>3000 BV</strong> to spend and <strong>3000 Support Points (SP)</strong>.</li>
                    <li>You must select at least one Mech. You may add a second Mech, a vehicle, or two Battle Armor units.</li>
                    <li>No aerospace units, no AE-damage units (artillery, Arrow IV), no Unique units.</li>
                    <li>All units must come from the same IlClan faction MUL. Experimental tech is allowed.</li>
                    <li>Each unit starts at <strong>Gunnery 4 / Piloting 5</strong> with <strong>150 XP</strong> to allocate. Battle Armor squads get 75 XP each.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# League Play Structure #}
    <div class="col-md-6">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white"><h5 class="mb-0">League Play Structure</h5></div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>Each month a <strong>Hot Spot contract</strong> is posted in Discord. Players pair up, choose one of the two opposing contracts, and complete it. Alternatively, roll a random contract via Dobless Information Services or the MRC Contract Generator (p. 15).</p>
                <p>A company may only receive credit for <strong>one contract per month</strong>.</p>
                <dl class="row mb-0">
                    <dt class="col-sm-4">Downtime</dt>
                    <dd class="col-sm-8">After each contract, players receive one month of downtime at a Hiring Hall of their choice. Downtime at a Hiring Hall costs a flat <strong>50 SP</strong> regardless of scale.</dd>
                    <dt class="col-sm-4">Overtime</dt>
                    <dd class="col-sm-8">If contracts for the month are complete, players may use the <em>Taking One for the Team</em> rules (HS:H p. 38) to run additional contracts with a new mercenary company.</dd>
                    <dt class="col-sm-4">Responsibilities</dt>
                    <dd class="col-sm-8">Players maintain their own Force record sheets. Incomplete contracts reset all accrued damage, salvage, and payments.</dd>
                </dl>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# Reputation & Salvage #}
    <div class="col-md-6">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white"><h5 class="mb-0">Reputation &amp; Salvage</h5></div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>All companies start with <strong>Reputation 1</strong>. Winning a full contract (at least half the tracks) gains +1 reputation. You never lose reputation for <em>losing</em> a contract, only for breaching it (–3) or abandoning objectives.</p>
                <p><strong>Spending Reputation:</strong> Spend 1 reputation to shift a RAT result by ±1, or to negate a Command Complication roll. You must keep at least 1 reputation to spend.</p>
                <p><strong>Salvage</strong> is the right to claim destroyed or crippled enemy units left on the field after a track. The victor may claim salvage if they successfully complete the track. Salvaged mechs are in their end-of-battle condition and must be repaired. If you decline salvage, the original owner may ransom the unit back.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# Salvaged Mechs #}
    <div class="col-12">
        <div class="card">
            <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white"><h5 class="mb-0">Salvaged Mechs</h5></div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>Salvaged mechs come from two sources: the <strong>scrapyard</strong> and <strong>battlefield salvage</strong>. Both pay repair costs in SP but differ in acquisition, condition, and contract interaction.</p>

                <h6 class="mt-3 mb-2">Scrapyard Mechs</h6>
                <p>When purchasing from the scrapyard, you pay <strong>half the mech's Battle Value (BV/2)</strong> as the acquisition cost. Scrapyard mechs always arrive in <strong>Crippled</strong> condition — they cannot be repaired to better than Crippled.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Acquisition cost:</strong> BV ÷ 2 (in SP)</li>
                    <li><strong>Repair cost:</strong> Tonnage × 3 (IS) or × 4.5 (Mixed/Clan) — since they're always Crippled</li>
                    <li><strong>BV for assignment:</strong> Full BV (as if undamaged)</li>
                    <li><strong>No contract attachment:</strong> Scrapyard mechs do not attach to active contracts, so salvage rights percentages are irrelevant</li>
                </ul>

                <h6 class="mb-2">Battlefield Salvage</h6>
                <p>After a track, the victor may attempt to salvage enemy units left on the field. A <strong>salvage check</strong> is made by rolling 2d6 with thresholds based on unit type: <strong>Mech: 4+</strong>, <strong>Vehicle: 6+</strong>, <strong>Battle Armor: 7+</strong>. Failure means the mech is destroyed — no salvage.</p>

                <p>If successful, the mech is acquired in its <strong>end-of-battle condition</strong>. The actual cost depends on your active contract's <strong>salvage rights</strong> terms:</p>

                <h6 class="mt-3 mb-2">Understanding Salvage Rights</h6>
                <p>Salvage rights is a contract term that determines how the cost of acquiring a salvaged mech is split between you and the employer:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Percentage (e.g., 50%, 75%, 100%):</strong> You keep that percentage of the salvage value. The acquisition cost is: <code>floor(BV/2) × (1 − salvageRightsPercent/100)</code>. A 50% salvage rights means you pay half the salvage value; 100% means you pay the full salvage value.</li>
                    <li><strong>Exchange:</strong> The employer covers all repair costs. You receive a payout of <strong>25% of the salvage value</strong> (floor(BV/2) × 0.25) as your share.</li>
                    <li><strong>None (0%):</strong> Acquisition is <strong>not allowed</strong> — you cannot claim the mech.</li>
                </ul>

                <p><strong>Salvage value</strong> (the base reference for all calculations) = <code>floor(BV ÷ 2)</code>. <strong>Repair cost</strong> is calculated separately based on the mech's damage state: Tonnage × 3 (Crippled, IS) or × 4.5 (Mixed/Clan), Tonnage × 5 (Destroyed, IS) or × 7.5 (Mixed/Clan), etc. Both the acquisition cost and repair cost must be paid in SP to bring the mech into your roster.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# Scale Limitations & Combat Pay #}
    <div class="col-md-6">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white"><h5 class="mb-0">Scale Limitations &amp; Combat Pay</h5></div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>The force you may deploy per track is capped by the contract's scale. In this league, the limit is <strong>3000 BV and 2 units per scale</strong>. Unit limits are checked once at the start of each contract.</p>
                <p>Infantry (battle armor, conventional) may be fielded at 2-for-1 against other unit slots, but this does not bypass the BV cap.</p>
                <p><strong>Maintenance:</strong> 500 SP × contract scale per month, paid regardless of whether a track is played.</p>
                <p><strong>Combat Pay</strong> is paid at the end of each track (500 SP × track scale):
                <ul class="mb-0">
                    <li>No objectives achieved → no pay</li>
                    <li>At least one objective, fewer Victory Points → half pay (250 × scale)</li>
                    <li>More Victory Points than opponent → full pay (500 × scale)</li>
                    <li>All objectives achieved → half-again pay (750 × scale)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# Force Scale Table #}
    <div class="col-md-6">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white"><h5 class="mb-0">Force Scale</h5></div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>The force scale determines the maximum force size and associated costs.</p>
                <div class="table-responsive">
                    <table class="table table-sm table-bordered mb-0">
                        <thead class="table-secondary">
                            <tr><th>Scale</th><th>Force</th><th>BV</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>Mixed Lance of 'Mechs and Support</td><td>3,000 BV</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>3</strong></td><td>Mixed Company</td><td>9,000 BV</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# Reputation Progression #}
    <div class="col-md-6">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white"><h5 class="mb-0">Reputation Progression &amp; Support Rating</h5></div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>Your company's reputation determines your force scale and military rank as you grow:</p>
                <div class="table-responsive mb-3">
                    <table class="table table-sm table-bordered mb-0">
                        <thead class="table-secondary">
                            <tr><th>Reputation</th><th>Scale</th><th>Rank</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>0–2</td><td>1</td><td>Sergeant / Point Commander</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>3–10</td><td>2</td><td>Lieutenant / Star Commander</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>11–20</td><td>3</td><td>Captain / Star Captain</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p><strong>Support Rating</strong> modifies contract terms based on your company's standing. A Support Rating of <strong>C</strong> gives a <strong>75%</strong> modifier. Losing reputation can cause you to lose force scale (but not rank).</p>
                <p class="mb-0">Winning a full contract (at least half the tracks) gains +1 reputation. You never lose reputation for losing a contract, only for breaching it (–3) or abandoning objectives.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# Special Rules #}
    <div class="col-md-6">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white"><h5 class="mb-0">Special Rules</h5></div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <dl class="row mb-0">
                    <dt class="col-sm-4">Commander</dt>
                    <dd class="col-sm-8">Each force designates one Commander unit per track (written down, not revealed). No initiative penalty if the Commander is destroyed.</dd>
                    <dt class="col-sm-4">Forced Withdrawal</dt>
                    <dd class="col-sm-8">Not in effect for player-generated forces. Crippled units may not perform pickup or scan actions. Forces without a company roster (opposing force table units) do use Forced Withdrawal.</dd>
                    <dt class="col-sm-4">Raid</dt>
                    <dd class="col-sm-8">Mechs carrying Jump MP components must make a PSR at +3 or lose the components. All units carrying components have Run/Flank MP reduced by 1.</dd>
                    <dt class="col-sm-4">Scanning</dt>
                    <dd class="col-sm-8">A unit within 2 hexes (AS 4") may declare a scan instead of firing. Roll 2d6 in the End Phase — 7+ succeeds. Modifiers: +2 (no physical attack), +2 (Active Probe within 2 hexes), +2 (started within 2 hexes), –2 (used jump/VTOL MP), –2 (fired weapons, unless carrying unjammed Active Probe). A successful scan forces the defender to reveal relevant unit information.</dd>
                </dl>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# Optional Rules #}
    <div class="col-md-6">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white"><h5 class="mb-0">Optional Rules</h5></div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>Used by mutual agreement. Veteran players should defer to less experienced players. Assumed active for MRC games:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Floating Critical Hits (BMM p. 45 / TO:AR p. 75)</li>
                    <li>Careful Stand (BMM p. 19 / TO:AR p. 22)</li>
                    <li>Skin-of-the-teeth Ejection (BMM p. 81 / TO:AR p. 165)</li>
                    <li>One-Armed Prone Fire (BMM p. 30 / TO:AR p. 83)</li>
                    <li>ECCM (TO:AR p. 98–99)</li>
                </ul>
                <p class="mb-0"><strong>Special Munitions</strong> and Command Consoles may also be used by agreement.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# Dobless Information Services #}
    <div class="col-12">
        <div class="card">
            <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white"><h5 class="mb-0">Dobless Information Services — Random Contracts</h5></div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>Players can generate random contracts by visiting the Almotacen Hiring Hall. Generate one random contract offer per month. If you don't like the contract, you must wait a month before generating another.</p>

                <h6 class="mt-3 mb-2">Jumping Between Systems</h6>
                <p>Roll 1D6 to determine the number of jumps from Almotacen to the contract system, then a second 1D6 to determine the specific system:</p>
                <div class="table-responsive mb-3">
                    <table class="table table-sm table-bordered">
                        <thead class="table-secondary"><tr><th>1D6</th><th>Number of Jumps to Contract System</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>1</td><td>1: Zoetermeer, 2: Baker 3, 3: Leskovik, 4: Dompaire, 5: Devin, 6: Antares</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>2</td><td>1: Babaeski, 2: Morges, 3: A Place, 4: Vulcan, 5: Parakoila, 6: Matteo</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>3</td><td>1: Bountiful Harvest, 2: Colmar, 3: Koniz, 4: Twycross, 5: Waldorff, 6: Romulus</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>4</td><td>1: New Exford, 2: Ballynure, 3: Zanderij, 4: Trell I, 5: Deia, 6: Great X</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>5</td><td>1: Santana, 2: Blumenort, 3: Kooken's Pleasure Pit, 4: Hot Springs, 5: Derf, 6: Timkovichi</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>6</td><td>1: Biuque, 2: Roadside, 3: Clermont, 4: Black Earth, 5: Golandrinas, 6: Sargasso</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h6 class="mb-2">Contract Generator</h6>
                <p>Contracts are generated in order: contract type → employer → pay rate → salvage rights → support terms → transportation → command rights. The employer type adds or subtracts steps from the rolled result.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# Vehicles and Infantry #}
    <div class="col-md-6">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white"><h5 class="mb-0">Vehicles &amp; Infantry</h5></div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <dl class="row mb-0">
                    <dt class="col-sm-4">Full Units</dt>
                    <dd class="col-sm-8">On the company roster with a pilot/crew. Pay all normal repair and training costs. May be salvaged. Conventional infantry is purchased in "barracks" — one barracks provides one unit per contract.</dd>
                    <dt class="col-sm-4">Support Units</dt>
                    <dd class="col-sm-8">Available at any track without being on the roster. Must be from the Periphery MUL, have no AE weapons, and be non-Mech/non-aerospace. Always 4/5 skill. Cannot be salvaged. Max 500 BV × scale.</dd>
                    <dt class="col-sm-4">BSAs</dt>
                    <dd class="col-sm-8">Battlefield Support Assets — optional for in-person or Tabletop Simulator play. Cannot be used alongside Support Units in the same track.</dd>
                </dl>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# Plotting the Monthly Schedule — The Dash Notation #}
    <div class="col-12">
        <div class="card">
            <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white"><h5 class="mb-0">Plotting the Monthly Schedule (The Dash Notation)</h5></div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>Once you know the total number of tracks and the length of the contract (usually 3 or 6 months), you roll <strong>1D6</strong> on the <em>Track Intensity Table</em>. This roll gives you a sequence of numbers separated by dashes (such as <code>0-1-1</code> or <code>0-1-0-1-1-0</code>). Each number represents the exact number of tracks you must deploy for in that specific month of the contract.</p>

                <h6 class="mt-3 mb-2">Example (3-Month Contract with 2 total tracks):</h6>
                <ul>
                    <li>If you roll a <strong>2</strong>, the intensity is <code>0-1-1</code>. You have a quiet Month 1 (no combat, just maintenance and training), followed by one battle in Month 2, and one battle in Month 3.</li>
                    <li>If you roll a <strong>5</strong>, the intensity is <code>0-0-2</code>. You face no combat for the first two months, but are hit with a double-header of two consecutive battles in Month 3.</li>
                </ul>

                <h6 class="mb-2">Campaign Implications: The Danger of "Double-Track" Months</h6>
                <p>Track Intensity heavily influences your logistics and repair planning. If a month's intensity is <strong>2</strong> (meaning two battles are fought in the same month):</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>No automatic full recovery:</strong> Your technicians will be rushed. Standard rules state that unless a unit undergoes Armor Only or non-crippling Omni unit repairs, any repaired unit is unavailable for the next track in that same month.</li>
                    <li><strong>Repair Time Rolls:</strong> To simulate this pressure, if there is a second track in the same month, you must roll <strong>2D6</strong> on the <em>Repair Time Table</em> between the two tracks. This determines if you have time for Hasty Repairs (only armor and rearming are allowed, and you must use the same units), Standard Repairs, Extended Repairs, or All the Time in the World (where full repairs and pilot healing can be safely completed before deploying again).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# Time Between Tracks #}
    <div class="col-12">
        <div class="card">
            <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white"><h5 class="mb-0">Time Between Tracks — SP Activity Costs</h5></div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <div class="table-responsive">
                    <table class="table table-sm table-bordered mb-3">
                        <thead class="table-secondary">
                            <tr><th>Activity</th><th>Cost</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Repair (Armor damage only)</td><td>Tonnage × 0.5 (IS) / × 0.75 (Mixed/Clan)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Repair (Structure/Critical damage)</td><td>Tonnage × 2 (IS) / × 3.5 (Mixed/Clan)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Repair (Crippled)</td><td>Tonnage × 3 (IS) / × 4.5 (Mixed/Clan)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Repair (Destroyed)</td><td>Tonnage × 5 (IS) / × 7.5 (Mixed/Clan)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Reconfigure Unit</td><td>Tonnage / 2</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Purchase Mech</td><td>Battle Value (Point Value × 40)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Sell a Mech</td><td>Battle Value / 2 (Point Value × 20)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Rearm (per ton of ammo)</td><td>10 (20 for special ammo)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Hire Unnamed MechWarrior/Crew</td><td>100</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Hire Named MechWarrior/Crew</td><td>150</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Heal Pilot</td><td>30 per Wound box (2 per month for AS)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Heal/Replace BA Trooper</td><td>20 per trooper lost</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Clan Doctrine Training</td><td>200</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <ul class="mb-0">
                    <li><strong>Repair Time:</strong> Repairing a unit makes it unavailable for the rest of the month.</li>
                    <li><strong>Truly Destroyed:</strong> A unit is truly destroyed only if its center torso internal structure is eliminated — it is gone permanently.</li>
                    <li><strong>Named Pilots:</strong> Max 4 on roster. Earn XP (1 SP per XP, up to 200 XP × scale per track). MVP of each track earns +20 bonus XP.</li>
                    <li><strong>Starting and Acquiring Edge:</strong> Every Named Pilot begins with a default rating of 1 Edge token. You can permanently increase a pilot's Edge rating by allocating Support Points (SP) earned from combat pay into the "Edge Tokens". Reaching specific SP milestones permanently grants additional tokens: accumulating 60 SP unlocks a second token, 120 SP unlocks a third, and 200 SP unlocks a fourth. Keep in mind that every Edge token a pilot holds also increases their Handicap value, which is used to balance matches against your opponent.</li>
                    <li><strong>Using Edge in Combat:</strong> Whenever a pilot begins a sortie, they enter the battlefield with a number of Edge tokens equal to their permanent Edge rating. During the track, you may spend these tokens to trigger the following effects:
                        <ul class="mb-0 mt-1">
                            <li><strong>Add to Attack Roll:</strong> After making an attack roll, you can spend one Edge token to add +1 to the final result. You are limited to spending a maximum of one token per attack. If you are playing <em>Alpha Strike</em>, using an Edge token to bump an attack roll of 11 up to a 12 does <em>not</em> trigger the automatic critical hit normally granted by a natural 12.</li>
                            <li><strong>Reroll Motive Systems Damage:</strong> If your vehicle takes a hit and a roll is made on the Motive Systems Damage Table, you can spend one Edge token to reroll it. You are forced to accept the second result, and you can only spend one token per motive system damage event.</li>
                            <li><strong>Reroll Critical Hit:</strong> If a roll is made against your unit on the Determining Critical Hits Table, you can spend one Edge token to completely reroll it. As with motive damage, you must accept the new result and can only use one token per critical hit roll.</li>
                            <li><strong>Activate Edge Abilities:</strong> You can spend Edge tokens to trigger any of the special <strong>Edge Abilities</strong> (such as <em>Coolant Flush</em>, <em>Assassin</em>, or <em>Nimble</em>) that your pilot has unlocked through training. You simply spend the number of Edge tokens equal to the ability's activation cost.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li><strong>Edge Abilities (SPAs):</strong> Abilities are unlocked progressively. A pilot unlocks a new ability slot when their allocated SP reaches 60, 180, 360, 600, and 900 SP. When a slot is unlocked, you permanently add an ability of your choice to that pilot.</li>
                    <ul class="mb-0 mt-1">
                        <li> <strong> Assassin</strong>: Spend 1 Edge before rolling a rear-arc attack to increase your damage rating by 1. For Total Warfare, you may add or subtract 1 from your hit location roll to try and strike rear torso armor.</li>
                        <li> <strong> Bulwark</strong>: Spend 1 Edge when taking physical attack damage (including self-inflicted damage from a Charge or Death From Above) to reduce the damage you receive by 1. For Total Warfare, reduce the damage by 5.</li>
                        <li><strong> Cautious</strong>: Spend 1 Edge at the start of the Combat Phase to instantly change your facing to any direction.</li>
                        <li><strong> Coolant Flush</strong>: Spend up to 2 Edge during the End Phase to reduce your heat by the same amount. For Total Warfare, reduce heat by 4 per Edge token spent.</li>
                        <li><strong> Forward Observer</strong>: Spend 1 Edge when spotting for another unit to make your own attack this Combat Phase without suffering the +1 Target Number penalty, preventing the penalty for any ally benefiting from your spotting.</li>
                        <li><strong> Jumping Jack</strong>: Spend 1 Edge when attacking a target at Short range after using jumping movement to reduce your jumping Target Number penalty to +1. For Total Warfare, reduces penalty to +2 instead of +3.</li>
                        <li><strong> Marksman</strong>: Spend 1 Edge after rolling an attack of 11 that hits to automatically inflict a critical hit. For Total Warfare, add 1 to your roll on the Determining Critical Hits Table.</li>
                        <li><strong> Melee Specialist</strong>: Spend 1 Edge after rolling a Physical or Melee attack to completely reroll the attack.</li>
                        <li><strong> Nimble</strong>: Spend 1 Edge when using ground or sprint movement to gain +1 Target Movement Modifier (TMM) and the ability to move through enemy units. If your sprinted TMM is 3 or more, or your Walking MP is 7+ in Total Warfare, this ability costs 2 Edge.</li>
                        <li><strong> Patient</strong>: Spend 1 Edge after rolling an attack to completely reroll all of your attacks, provided your unit used Standstill movement or did not move this turn.</li>
                        <li><strong> Protector</strong>: Spend 1 Edge when a friendly, smaller unit within 2 inches or 1 hex takes damage to prevent 1 damage to that unit. For Total Warfare, it prevents the entire damage from that specific weapon.</li>
                        <li><strong> Speed Demon</strong>: Spend 1 Edge when using ground or sprint movement to gain +2 inches of movement this turn, or +4 inches if sprinting. For Total Warfare, gain 1 MP, or 2 MP if sprinting.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <li><strong>Unnamed Pilots:</strong> Always cost 100 SP to hire, always Gunnery 4 / Piloting 5, cannot earn XP.</li>
                    <li><strong>Handicap:</strong> Each named pilot has a handicap based on skill improvements. The player with the lower average handicap earns a BSP bonus equal to the handicap difference × 10.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# Dropship Mechanics #}
    <div class="col-12">
        <div class="card">
            <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white"><h5 class="mb-0">Dropship Mechanics</h5></div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>Your company gets <strong>one dropship</strong> (unique per company at the DB level). It serves as your mobile repair bay — you must board salvaged mechs onto the dropship to repair them using Support Points (SP).</p>

                <h6 class="mt-3 mb-2">Two Limits: Tonnage + Mekbays</h6>
                <p>When assigning roster units (active battlemechs) to the dropship, both limits must be satisfied:</p>

                <div class="table-responsive mb-3">
                    <table class="table table-sm table-bordered">
                        <thead class="table-secondary">
                            <tr><th>LIMIT</th><th>WHAT IT TRACKS</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Tonnage (maxCapacity)</strong></td><td>Total tons of all units on board (roster + salvage)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Mekbays (mekbayCapacity)</strong></td><td>Number of roster units currently aboard (each unit = 1 mekbay)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <p>Salvaged mechs only consume tonnage — they don't use mekbays. This is intentional: salvaged mechs are stored in cargo holds, not repair bays.</p>

                <p>When you board a roster unit, the system checks:</p>
                <ol>
                    <li><code>currentTonnage + unit.tonnage ≤ maxCapacity</code></li>
                    <li><code>usedMekbays < mekbayCapacity</code></li>
                </ol>

                <h6 class="mt-3 mb-2">Creating a Dropship</h6>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Minimum 40 tons</strong> (allows at least 2 × 20-ton mechs)</li>
                    <li><strong>Initial mekbay capacity starts at 0</strong> (you can set higher on creation)</li>
                    <li>You specify <strong>name</strong>, <strong>maxCapacity</strong>, and <strong>mekbayCapacity</strong> when creating</li>
                </ul>

                <h6 class="mt-3 mb-2">Upgrading: 200 SP Per Upgrade</h6>
                <p>You can upgrade your dropship by purchasing additional capacity for <strong>200 SP</strong> each:</p>

                <div class="table-responsive mb-3">
                    <table class="table table-sm table-bordered">
                        <thead class="table-secondary">
                            <tr><th>UPGRADE OPTION</th><th>EFFECT</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>+30 tons</strong></td><td>Increase maxCapacity by 30</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>+1 mekbay</strong></td><td>Increase mebayCapacity by 1</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <p>Each upgrade costs 200 SP — you pay SP from your company balance to increase either the tonnage or mekbay count (or both, at 200 SP each).</p>

                <h6 class="mt-3 mb-2">Why Board Salvage to the Dropship?</h6>
                To repair them, you must:</p>
                <ol>
                    <li><strong>Board them onto your dropship</strong> (costs tonnage only)</li>
                    <li><strong>Pay the repair SP</strong> (based on damage state × tonnage × IS/Clan multiplier)</li>
                </ol>
                <p>The dropship is your only repair facility — without it, salvaged mechs cannot be repaired.</p>

                <h6 class="mt-3 mb-2">Key Rules</h6>
                <ul class="mb-0">
                    <li>Only one dropship per company</li>
                    <li>You can unassign mech/units at any time (free)</li>
                    <li>You can delete the dropship (but all mechs are unassigned first)</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

